"Up to \"me\" or up to \"us\"? The impact of self-construal priming on cognitive self-other integration - PubMed (https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23049518/)\nciteturn2search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 14.1 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; doi: 10.3389/fpsyg.2012.00341. eCollection 2012. ...   * DOI: 10.3389/fpsyg.2012.00341 ... As predicted, the SSE was more pronounced in the interdependence group than in the independence group, suggesting that self-other integration varies dynamically as a function of the relative saliency of the other.\n\n. 2012 Sep 14:3:341.\n\ndoi: 10.3389/fpsyg.2012.00341. eCollection 2012.\n\n# Up to \"me\" or up to \"us\"? The impact of self-construal priming on cognitive self-other integration\n\nLorenza S Colzato^{  1 }, Ellen R A de Bruijn, Bernhard Hommel\n\nAffiliations Expand\n\n### Affiliation\n\n  * ^{1} Institute for Psychological Research, Leiden University Leiden, Netherlands ; Leiden Institute for Brain and Cognition, Leiden University Leiden, Netherlands.\n\n  * PMID: 23049518\n  * PMCID: PMC3442283\n  * DOI: 10.3389/fpsyg.2012.00341\n\nItem in Clipboard\n\n# Up to \"me\" or up to \"us\"? The impact of self-construal priming on cognitive self-other integration\n\nLorenza S Colzato et al. Front Psychol. 2012.\n\n### Authors\n\nLorenza S Colzato^{  1 }, Ellen R A de Bruijn, Bernhard Hommel\n\n### Affiliation\n\n  * ^{1} Institute for Psychological Research, Leiden University Leiden, Netherlands ; Leiden Institute for Brain and Cognition, Leiden University Leiden, Netherlands.\n\n  * PMID: 23049518\n  * PMCID: PMC3442283\n  * DOI: 10.3389/fpsyg.2012.00341\n\n## Abstract\n\nThe degree to which people construe their perceived self as independent from or interdependent with their social environment can vary. We tested whether the current degree of social self-construal predicts the degree to which individuals integrate others into their self-concept. Participants worked through tasks that drew attention to either personal interdependence (e.g., by instructing participants to circle all relational pronouns in a text, such as \"we,\" \"our,\" or \"us\") or independence (by having them to circle pronouns such as \"I,\" \"my,\" or \"me\") and were compared with respect to the social Simon effect (SSE) - an index of the degree to which people co-represent the actions of a co-actor. As predicted, the SSE was more pronounced in the interdependence group than in the independence group, suggesting that self-other integration varies dynamically as a function of the relative saliency of the other.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nUS 2012.00341 60A1 (https://patentimages.storage.googleapis.com/c5/90/f9/a168f990367180/US20120034160A1.pdf)\nciteturn2search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.3 years ago; US 2012.00341 60A1\nUS 2012.00341 60A1 \n(19) United States \n(12) Patent Application Publication (10) Pub. No.: US 2012/0034160 A1 \nGhayur et al. \n(43) Pub. Date: \nFeb. 9, 2012 \n(54) DUAL VARIABLE DOMAIN \nA6IP37/08 \n(2006.01) \nMMUNOGLOBULINS AND USES THEREOF \nC07K 6/24 \n(2006.01) \nA6IPI/00 \n2006.O1 \n(75) Inventors: \nTariq Ghayur, (US); Junjian Liu, \nC7H 2L/04 \n308: \n(US); Peter C. Isakson, (US) \nA6IP3 L/16 \n(2006.01) \nA6IP 25/06 \n2006.O1 \n(73) Assignee: \nABBOTT LABORATORIES \nA6IP 9/10 \n:08: \nA6IP 700 \n(2006.01) \n(21) Appl. No.: \n13/196,138 \nA6IP 25/00 \n(2006.01) \nA6IP 25/18 \n2006.O1 \n(22) Filed: \nAug. 2, 2011 \nGOIN 33/566 \n308: \nRelated U.S. Application Data \n39t. ge \n308: \n(60) Provisional application No. 61/370.269, filed on Aug. \nA6IP 9/00 \n(2006.01) \n3, 2010, provisional application No. 61/377,134, filed \nA6IP 5/00 \n(2006.01) \non Aug. 26, 2010. \nA6IP37/02 \n(2006.01) \nA6IP35/02 \n(2006.01) \nPublication Classification \nA6IP35/00 \n(2006.01) \nA6IP3L/04 \n(2006.01) \n(51) Int. Cl. \nA6 IK 5L/It \n(2006.01) \nA6IP 29/00 \n(2006.01) \nA6IP4/00 \n(2006.01) \nA6IP 7/02 \n(2006.01) \nA6IP3 L/18 \n(2006.01) \nA6IP