$. Numerical semigroups with $t=2g-F$ are called almost symmetric, we introduce a new property that characterises them. We give an explicit characterisation of numerical semigroups with $t=\\frac{g}{F+1-g}$. We show that for a fixed $\\alpha$ the number of numerical semigroups with Frobenius number F and type $F-\\alpha$ is eventually constant for large F. Also the number of numerical semigroups with genus g and type $g-\\alpha$ is also eventually constant for large g.\nDate: June 2020.\n\n1\n\narXiv:2008.08110v1 [math.CO] 18 Aug 2020\n\n2 DEEPESH SINGHAL\n\nFor a numerical semigroup S we deﬁne it’s T-Set to be\n\nT(S) = {x∈N|F(S)−x∈((Z\\S)∪ {0})}.\n\nNote that T(S) is a subset of natural numbers, it contains 0 and has\n\na ﬁnite complement with respect to N. However it is not necessarily\n\nclosed under addition, such sets are called numerical sets. In fact it is\n\nclosed under addition pricisely when S is almost symmetric, in Section\n\n2 we prove that\n\nTheorem 1.1. A numerical semigroup is almost symmetric if and only\n\nif its T-Set is itself a numerical semigroup.\n\nIf S is a numerical semigroup then A?(S) = S∪P F (S) is another\n\nnumerical semigroup. In [5] it is proven that\n\nTheorem 1.2. [5, Theorem 3.7] Given an almost symmetric numerical\n\nsemigroup S,A?(S)is almost symmetric if and only if t(A?(S)) =\n\nm(S)−t(S).\n\nBy using T-Sets we ﬁnd another equivalent condition for this to\n\nhappen, we prove in Corollary 4.8.3 that\n\nTheorem 1.3. If S is an almost symmetric numerical semigroup then\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nORIGINAL RESEARCH ARTICLE (https://citeseerx.ist.psu.edu/document?doi=64091a150787a358c5d18a97d402829b7d34ab50&repid=rep1&type=pdf)\nciteturn2search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8 months ago; ORIGINAL RESEARCH ARTICLE\npublished: 14 September 2012\ndoi: 10.3389/fpsyg.2012.00341 ... group, suggesting that self-other integration varies dynamically as a function of the relative\nOR