v 19:3:341.\n\ndoi: 10.3389/fimmu.2012.00341. eCollection 2012.\n\n### Authors\n\nConrad A Farrar^{  1 }, Elham Asgari, Wilhelm J Schwaeble, Steven H Sacks\n\n### Affiliation\n\n  * ^{1} MRC Centre for Transplantation, Division of Transplantation Immunology and Mucosal Biology, King's College London School of Medicine at Guy's, King's College and St Thomas' Hospitals London, UK.\n\n  * PMID: 23181062\n  * PMCID: PMC3500775\n  * DOI: 10.3389/fimmu.2012.00341\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTidying up the peritoneal dialysis modality classification: a proposal. (https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24133087/)\nciteturn2search4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 months ago;   * DOI: 10.3747/pdi.2012.00341\n# Tidying up the peritoneal dialysis modality classification: a proposal.\n\nPeritoneal dialysis international : journal of the International Society for Peritoneal Dialysis\n\nPMID: 24133087\n\nPublication date: 2013 Sep-Oct\n\nJournal abbreviation: Perit Dial Int\n\nJournal country: United States\n\nJournal NLM unique ID: 8904033\n\nJournal linking ISSN: 0896-8608\n\nISSN: 1718-4304\n\nVolume: 33\n\nIssue: 5\n\nPages: 581-2\n\n## Identifiers\n\n  * DOI: 10.3747/pdi.2012.00341\n  * PII: 33/5/581\n  * PMC: PMC3797681\n\n\n  * P Katavetin\n  * N Saiprasertkit\n  * T Kanjanabuch\n\n## Affiliations\n\n  * Division of Nephrology, Department of Medicine King Chulalongkorn Memorial Hospital Faculty of Medicine, Chulalongkorn University Bangkok, Thailand.\n\n## Languages\n\n  * eng\n\n## Publication types\n\n  * Letter\n\n## MeSH terms\n\n  * Humans\n  * Kidney Failure, Chronic / therapy\n  * Peritoneal Dialysis / classification\n  * Terminology as Topic--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nau:Upadhyay_A in:math - SciRate Search (https://scirate.com/search?q=au%3AUpadhyay_A+in%3Amath)\nciteturn2search5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; In a recent paper Dave Benson and Peter Symonds defined a new invariant $\\gamma_G(M)$ for a finite dimensional module $M$ of a finite group $G$ which attempts to quantify how close a module is to being projective. In this paper, we determine this invariant for all the signed permutation modules of the symmetric group using tools from representation theory and combinatorics. ... Dec 02 2020 math.RT math.CO arXiv:2012.00341v1\n\n[Button: Scited][Button: Scite!]\n\n0\n\n@misc{2101.10612, author = {Aparna Upadhyay}, title = {{T}he {B}enson -- {S}ymonds {I}nvariant for {O}rdinary and {S}igned {P}ermutation {M}odules}, year = {2021}, eprint = {2101.10612}, note = {arXiv:2101.10612v1} }[Button: Copy Citation]\n\nPDF\n\nThe signed permutation modules are a simultaneous generalization of the ordinary permutation modules and the twisted permutation modules of the symmetric group. In a recent paper Dave Benson and Peter Symonds defined a new invariant $\\gamma_G(M)$ for a finite dimensional module $M$ of a finite group $G$ which attempts to quantify how close a module is to being projective. In this paper, we determine this invariant for all the signed permutation modules of the symmetric group using tools from representation theory and combinatorics.\n\n  * The Benson-Symonds Invariant for Permutation Modules\n\nAparna Upadhyay\n\nDec 02 2020 math.RT math.CO arXiv:2012.00341v1\n\n[Button: Scited][Button: Scite!]\n\n0\n\n@misc{2012.00341, author = {Aparna Upadhyay}, title = {{T}he {B}enson-{S}ymonds {I}nvariant for {P}ermutation {M}odules}, year = {2020}, eprint = {2012.00341}, note = {arXiv:2012.00341v1} }[Button: Copy Citation]\n\nPDF\n\nIn a recent paper, Dave Benson and Peter Symonds defined a new invariant $\\gamma_G(M)$ for a finite dimensional module $M$ of a finite group $G$ which attempts to quantify how close a module is to being projective. In this paper, we determine this invariant for permutation modules of the symmetric group corresponding to two-part partitions using tools from representation theory and combinatorics.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInfinite families of 3-designs from APN functions (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/332300713_Infinite_families_of_3-designs_from_APN_functions)\nciteturn2search6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 5 months ago; DOI:10.48550/arXiv.1904.04071 ...   * This person is not on ResearchGate, or hasn't claimed this research yet. ... Combinatorial t-designs have nice applications in coding theory, finite geometries and several engineering areas. ... The objective of this paper is to study how to obtain 3-designs with 2-transitive permutation groups. ... A characterization of such incidence structure to be a 3-design is presented, and a sufficient condition for the stabilizer of a base block to be trivial is given.\nPreprint\n\n# Infinite families of 3-designs from APN functions\n\n  * April 2019\n\nDOI:10.48550/arXiv.1904.04071\n\nAuthors:\n\nChunming Tang\n\nChunming Tang\n\n  * This person is not on ResearchGate, or hasn't claimed this research yet.\n\nImage: Request Full-text Paper PDF\n\n## Abstract\n\nCombinatorial t-designs have nice applications in coding theory, finite geometries and several engineering areas. A classical method for constructing t-designs is by the action of a permutation group that is t-transitive or t-homogeneous on a point set. This approach produces t-designs, but may not yield (t+1)-designs. The objective of this paper is to study how to obtain 3-designs with 2-transitive permutation groups. The incidence structure formed by the orbits of a base block under the action of the general affine groups, which are 2-transitive, is considered. A characterization of such incidence structure to be a 3-design is presented, and a sufficient condition for the stabilizer of a base block to be trivial is given. With these general results, infinite families of 3-designs are constructed by employing APN functions. Some 3-designs presented in this paper give rise to self-dual binary codes or linear codes with optimal or best parameters known. Several conjectures on 3-designs and binary codes are also presented.\n\n## No file available\n\nImage: Request Full-text Paper PDF\n\nTo read the file of this research,\nyou can request a copy directly from the author.\n\nRequest file PDF--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPrime realization conjecture for exceptional harmonic-sum values — MathDB (https://mathdb.com/p/347789/prime-realization-conjecture-for-exceptional-harmonic-sum-va)\nciteturn2search7 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; Prime realization conjecture for exceptional harmonic-sum values ... For a prime $p$p, let condition (4.1) be the harmonic-sum congruence defined in the paper, and count the integers $d$d with $d\\leq p-1$d≤p−1 for which that condition is not satisfied. ... source: Karl Dilcher and Maciej Ulas, “Divisibility and Arithmetic Properties of a Class of Sparse Polynomials”, arXiv:2008.13475 (2021).\n\nPrime realization conjecture for exceptional harmonic-sum values\n\nConjecture open\n\nFor a prime $p$p, let condition (4.1) be the harmonic-sum congruence defined in the paper, and count the integers $d$d with $d\\leq p-1$d≤p−1 for which that condition is not satisfied. Prime realization conjecture. For every integer $k\\geq 1$k≥1, there is a prime number $P_k$P k​ such that condition (4.1) does not hold for exactly $k$k values of $d\\leq P_k-1$d≤P k​−1. Numerical computations find such primes for $k\\leq 8$k≤8, but the general assertion is refuted.\n\n## Progress summary\n\nRefresh\n\nNothing recorded yet. Refresh searches the literature and the public web for attempts on this problem, and writes the first summary here.\n\nsource: Karl Dilcher and Maciej Ulas, “Divisibility and Arithmetic Properties of a Class of Sparse Polynomials”, arXiv:2008.13475 (2021).\n\ncongruencesnumber-theoryprime-numbers\n\n0\n\nposted by arXiv 2 days ago\n\n\n## 0 Replies\n\n* * *\n\nSi